    <!-- Help page. Wording must match Hooker.java: an empty allowlist lets
         everything through, a non-empty one only lets the listed apps through,
         a match gets a 2000ms temporary exemption plus
         FLAG_INCLUDE_STOPPED_PACKAGES; list edits apply at once, enabling or
         updating the module needs one reboot. -->
    <string name="help_section_how">About this app</string>
    <string name="help_how_body">HyperOS power policy throttles background broadcasts and network access. Push (FCM) works by having GMS deliver a broadcast that wakes the app; if that broadcast is deferred or dropped, the app never sees the message.\n\nThis module changes those decisions inside system_server: it lets the push and reconnect broadcasts through, keeps GMS from being cut off, frozen or blacklisted, and on every push it grants the target app a temporary ~2s power exemption and lets the broadcast reach apps that are in the stopped state.</string>
    <string name="help_section_allowlist">The allowlist mechanism</string>
    <string name="help_allowlist_empty_title">Nothing checked</string>
    <string name="help_allowlist_empty_body">Equivalent to letting everything through: any app that receives a push may be auto-started and woken, with no list to maintain. The cost is noticeably higher background battery and memory use than checking only a few apps.</string>
    <string name="help_allowlist_checked_title">Some apps checked</string>
    <string name="help_allowlist_checked_body">Only those apps are affected and can be woken by a push. Everything unchecked falls back to the stock system behaviour: pushes may be deferred, batched, or only delivered once you open the app yourself. You have to maintain the list — miss an app and its pushes are delayed.</string>
    <string name="help_section_faq">Troubleshooting</string>
    <string name="help_faq_q1">I checked an app but pushes still do not arrive</string>
    <string name="help_faq_a1">Check in order: (1) the module is enabled in LSPosed and its scope includes System Framework; (2) the device was rebooted once after enabling or updating the module; (3) the app really uses FCM; (4) use FCM Diagnostics to check the GMS connection state.</string>
    <string name="help_faq_q2">Do I need to reboot after editing the list?</string>
    <string name="help_faq_a2">No. The change notifies the module inside the system immediately and it re-reads the list (as long as the module is enabled with the right scope). Only the first enable — or an update of the module — needs one reboot.</string>
    <string name="help_faq_q3">Battery use went up</string>
    <string name="help_faq_a3">That is inherent to the module: it lets push reconnects through and allows apps to be woken. To save power, switch to allowlist mode and check only the apps whose pushes must be instant — the narrower the list, the smaller the cost.</string>
    <string name="help_faq_q4">How is “supported apps” decided?</string>
    <string name="help_faq_a4">It looks for Firebase / GMS push receiver class names in each app’s manifest, which is a heuristic — an unusual implementation can be missed. The mark only helps you filter; checking an app is never limited by it.</string>
</resources>
